From: uca443e6eaa0e78ed at groups.google.com (Joe Cheng)
Date: Tue, 14 May 2013 07:47:56 +0000
Subject: Re: system() does not seem to work within a shiny app... why is this?
Message-ID: <1PZ4_y6s6sk.rlqRwxqODvMJ@shiny-discuss.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <1PZ4_y6s6sk.ME4f30F6NpoJ@shiny-discuss.groups.google.com>
References: <1PZ4_y6s6sk.ME4f30F6NpoJ@shiny-discuss.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/shiny-discuss/c/1PZ4_y6s6sk/m/rlqRwxqODvMJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It's because reactive expressions are lazily evaluated; since systemCall is never used by anyone, its code is never executed.

You can create an observer to get the code to execute:

observe({ systemCall() })

Or just replace systemCall altogether with:

observe({

  input$txt
  system('ls > tmpFile.txt')
})

You can read more about reactive expressions (aka conductors) and observers (aka endpoints) in the "Reactivity Overview" chapter of the tutorial:
http://rstudio.github.io/shiny/tutorial/#reactivity-overview

